WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.510
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:09.280
When using cryptography, we'd like to safely
communicate over the internet in the presence

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.959
of third parties.

00:00:10.959 --> 00:00:16.240
To be able to do this, and many other important
applications, we need random numbers.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.830
But what does it exactly mean that something
is random? Randomness is the lack of any patterns

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.869
and predictability.

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:29.699
People usually use coinflips as random events.
But is a coinflip really random? If we had

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:34.829
a really smart physicist, who can model all
the forces that act upon the coin, he would

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:40.280
easily find out whether it's going to be heads
or tails. Strictly speaking, a coinflip is

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.949
therefore not random.

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:48.250
What about random numbers generated with computers?
Computers are a collection of processing units

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:53.149
that run programs. If one knows the program
code that generates the random numbers, they

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:59.370
are not random anymore, because it doesn't
happen by chance, and it is possible to predict.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:04.409
John von Neumann famously said:
"Any one who considers arithmetical methods

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:10.869
of producing random digits is, of course,
in a state of sin. For, as has been pointed

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:16.000
out several times, there is no such thing
as a random number — there are only methods

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.229
to produce random numbers, and a strict arithmetic
procedure of course is not such a method."

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:27.400
Some websites offer high quality random numbers
that are generated from atmospheric noise.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.310
Practically speaking, this, of course, sounds
adequate enough! If someone wants to break

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:36.619
the encryption of our communications, they
would have to be able to model the physics

00:01:36.619 --> 00:01:42.700
and initial conditions of every single thunderbolt,
which means processing millions of discharges

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.280
per day. This is practically impossible.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.329
So it seems reasonable to say that random
events are considered random because of our

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:55.759
ignorance, not because they are, strictly
speaking, unpredictable. You just need to

00:01:55.759 --> 00:02:01.250
be smart enough, and the notion of randomness
fades away in the light of your intelligence.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.289
Or so it seemed for physicists for a long
time.

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:10.070
Imagine if someone who has never heard about
magnetism would see many magnets attracting

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:15.710
each other and some added magnet powder. This
person would most definitely say it's magic

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:16.540
happening.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.510
However, if you know about magnetism, you
know that things don't happen randomly, there

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.070
are very simple laws that can predict all
this movement.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:31.160
In this case, magnetic forces we can loosely
call a hidden variable. So we have a phenomenon

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.410
that we cannot predict, and we're keen to
say it's random. In reality, it is not, there

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:41.190
is just a hidden variable that we don't know
of, that is responsible for this behavior.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:46.810
We have the very same phenomenon if we look
inside of an atom. Quantum-level effects happen

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:52.130
according to the physics of extremely small
things, and we again, find behaviors that

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:57.480
seem completely random. We know some of the
trends, just like we know which roads in our

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:02.510
city are expected to have a huge traffic jam
every morning, but we cannot predict where

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:08.050
every single individual car is heading. We
have it the same way with extremely small particles.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.300
We're keen to say
that a behavior seems completely random, because

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.900
nothing that we know or measure would explain
it. Other people will immediately say, wait

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.240
- you don't know everything, maybe these quantum
effects are not random, as there may be hidden

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.700
things, hidden variables that you don't know
of, which make up for the behavior.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:33.180
We can't just say this or that is random - it
is much, much more likely that our knowledge

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:38.530
is insufficient to predict what is happening,
as electromagnetic forces seemed magical to

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:41.290
scientists a few hundred years ago.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:46.240
So is quantum mechanics completely random,
or does it only seem random?

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:51.610
It is probably one of the most difficult questions
ever asked. How can you find out that something

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:57.400
you measure that seems random, is really completely
random, and not just the act of forces that

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.120
you don't know of?

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.390
And hold on to your chair, because this is
going to blow your mind. A simple and intuitive

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:07.470
statement of Bell's theorem states that.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:13.380
"No physical theory of local hidden variables
can ever reproduce all of the predictions

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.110
of quantum mechanics."

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:20.060
This means that he proved that the behavior
scientists experience in quantum mechanics

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:26.620
are really random, they cannot be explained
by any theory you could possibly make up.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.530
Simple one or complicated, doesn't matter.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.800
This discovery is absolutely insane.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.200
You can definitely prove that a crappy theory
someone quickly made up doesn't explain a

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.840
behavior, but how can you prove that it is
completely impossible to build such a theory

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.540
that does? No matter how hard you try, how
smart you are, you can't do it.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.840
This is such a mind-bogglingly awesome theorem.
And please note that we definitely lose out

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.990
on some details and generality because of
the fact that we use intuitive words to discuss

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:03.790
these results, as opposed to the original
derivation with covariances between measurements.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:08.370
On our imaginary list of
the wonders of the world, monuments created

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:14.380
not by the hands, but the minds of humans,
this should definitely be among the best them.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.540
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

